export const S6={title:'Job shadowing and the learning outcomes summary',hook:'Eight hours inside a real workplace teach things no website can: the pace, the teamwork, the small rituals of safety. Clinical Specialty asks you to document those hours on a signed form and then turn them into a one-page reflection worth 10 points.',objectives:['Arrange and complete at least 8 hours of job shadowing professionally.','Complete the in-person or virtual Work-based Learning Form correctly.','Protect confidentiality and act professionally in clinical settings.','Write a one-page learning outcomes summary meeting all five criteria.'],sections:[
['Requirements for the work-based learning experience',[
'Competitors complete a **work-based learning experience (job shadow)** of **at least 8 hours** with a professional in the chosen career, documented on HOSA’s **Work-based Learning Form**. The form is **completed by the health professional shadowed**, records the dates, check-in and check-out times and total hours, and includes the host’s name, title, company and signature. Virtual shadowing may be acceptable in some cases; the **virtual form** also records the platform used, and 2026–27 adds an **in-person** form. Hours may be spread across more than one visit. On the rating sheet (5 points, all or nothing), the form must completely document **(1) at least 8 hours**, **(2) the host’s name, title and location**, **(3) an evaluation of student performance** and **(4) the host’s signature**; missing or questionable information earns 2 points.',
chk('Which Work-based Learning Form earns full points?',['8.5 hours across two visits, host name, title and location, completed evaluation and host signature','6 hours with a signature','8 hours signed by the competitor','10 hours with no host title or evaluation'],'All four items are required.')]],
['What the host evaluates',[
'The form asks the host to rate five objectives as **exceeds**, **met** or **needs improvement**: whether the competitor **effectively explained HOSA** to the host, **communicated professionally** to set up the experience (email, phone or in person), **demonstrated punctuality**, was **professionally attired** and **conducted themselves professionally**—positive attitude, engagement, prepared with strong questions and **not on the phone**. Hosts may add optional advice. Prepare a 30-second explanation of HOSA (a career and technical student organization for future health professionals with competitive events, leadership and service), arrive 10–15 minutes early, follow the site’s dress code (often closed-toe shoes, no strong fragrance, hair tied back, minimal jewelry) and keep your phone away.',
sort('Professional or unprofessional during shadowing?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['Arriving 10 minutes early',0],['Asking prepared questions at appropriate times',0],['Texting during a patient conversation',1],['Wearing open-toed sandals in a clinical area',1],['Briefly explaining HOSA to the host',0],['Taking a photo of a patient chart',1],['Stepping out when a patient prefers privacy',0],['Touching equipment without permission',1]])]],
['Confidentiality and safety at the site',[
'Many sites require observers to complete paperwork, a confidentiality agreement, proof of immunizations or a background check before shadowing, so begin early. Under **HIPAA**, anything you see or hear about patients is confidential: do not take photos, discuss patients outside the site or write identifying details in your portfolio. Follow infection-control rules—hand hygiene, staying out of isolation rooms unless permitted and wearing any PPE provided. Observers **do not provide patient care**; offer to help only with tasks the host invites, and step out whenever a patient declines an observer.',
kase('A request at the bedside',{a:{text:'While shadowing a physical therapist, a patient asks you to help them stand. What should you do?',choices:[['Politely explain you are an observer and alert the therapist','b','Correct. Observers do not perform patient care.',true],['Help the patient stand quickly','b','You could injure the patient or yourself; this is outside your role.',false]]},b:{text:'Later, you see a patient you recognize from school. What do you do?',choices:[['Keep it confidential and never mention it to anyone','c','Correct. HIPAA expectations apply to observers.',true],['Tell classmates you saw them','c','Sharing is a privacy violation.',false]]},c:{text:'The therapist thanks you for handling both situations professionally and notes it on your form.',end:true}})]],
['Observing with purpose',[
'Go in with an observation plan so your learning outcomes summary has substance. Notice the **work environment** (setting, pace, noise, physical demands), **teamwork** (who the professional communicates with and how), **technology** (equipment, electronic health records), **patient interactions** (how they explain, comfort and educate), **safety practices** (identification, hand hygiene) and how your **selected skill** is performed in real life. Take brief, nonidentifying notes during breaks and write a reflection at the end of each day.',
flip('Observation prompts',[['Environment','What is the pace? What physical demands do you see?'],['Teamwork','Who does the professional collaborate with, and how do they hand off information?'],['Technology','Which devices and software are used most often?'],['Patient interaction','How does the professional explain procedures and respond to concerns?'],['Your skill','How does real practice compare with the textbook steps?'],['Fit','What parts of the day matched your strengths? What surprised you?']])]],
['The learning outcomes summary (maximum one page)',[
'This 10-point section is a **complete, clear and comprehensive narrative** with five criteria: **(1)** a description of **who, what, where and when** the experience took place; **(2)** **insight and understanding** of the work environment and career; **(3)** a **thoughtful summary of learning outcomes**—what you learned; **(4)** **alignment** with the chosen career; and **(5)** **one page only, including the subtitle**. Including four criteria with some superficial information earns 8; three earns 4; two earns 2. A strong summary opens with the facts (host, setting, dates, hours), explains two or three specific insights with examples, describes how your view of the career changed and closes by connecting the experience to your goals.',
order('Organize a one-page learning outcomes summary',['Subtitle and the who, what, where and when of the experience','Insight into the work environment with a specific example','Insight into the career’s responsibilities and teamwork','What you learned, including about your selected skill','How the experience confirmed or changed your career goals']),
chk('A learning outcomes summary is a page and a half. What happens?',['It fails the one-page criterion, lowering the score','It earns bonus points for detail','Nothing; length is not scored','Judges read only the first half page'],'The summary is one page only, including the subtitle.')]],
["From observation to insight",["The difference between an average and an excellent learning outcomes summary is **insight**. A list of activities (“I watched three evaluations and a team meeting”) describes; insight interprets (“Watching three evaluations showed me that therapists adjust each plan to the patient’s goals—one patient wanted to garden again, so the therapist built kneeling practice into the session”). For each observation, ask: *What did I see? Why was it done that way? What does that reveal about the career? How does it connect to me?*","Use precise, professional language and keep patient details general. Mention the **work environment**—pace, physical demands, teamwork, technology—because the rubric names it, and include at least one realistic challenge of the career (shift work, emotional demands, documentation load) to show balanced understanding. End by connecting the experience to your goals and to the skill you chose. Keep everything to one page, including the subtitle, so choose the two or three strongest insights rather than everything you saw.",{"t":"example","title":"Turning description into insight","prompt":"Description: “I watched the dental hygienist clean teeth and take X-rays for 8 hours.” Rewrite it as an insight sentence.","steps":["Name a specific moment.","Explain the reason behind the professional’s action.","State what this reveals about the career."],"answer":"“When an anxious patient gripped the chair, the hygienist paused, explained each instrument and agreed on a hand signal for breaks; I learned that managing fear is as central to dental hygiene as technical skill.”"},{"t":"check","q":"Which sentence shows insight rather than description?","options":["“Because the therapist adjusted each plan to patient goals, I learned that rehabilitation is individualized.”","“I was there from 8 to 4.”","“I saw many patients.”","“The clinic had a waiting room.”"],"why":"Insight explains why and what it reveals."}]]
],takeaways:['Shadow at least 8 hours; the host completes and signs the form with name, title, location and evaluation.','Hosts rate explaining HOSA, professional communication, punctuality, attire and conduct (no phone).','The learning outcomes summary is one page: who/what/where/when, insight, what you learned and alignment with the career.'],terms:[['Job shadowing','Observing a professional at work to learn about a career.'],['Observer','A visitor who watches but does not provide care.'],['Learning outcome','A specific understanding gained from an experience.']],mcqs:[q('What is the minimum job shadowing time?','8 hours','2 hours','20 hours','4 hours','The form documents at least 8 hours.'),q('Who completes the Work-based Learning Form?','The health professional shadowed','The competitor','The HOSA advisor','A parent','It must be completed by the host.'),q('Which is one of the objectives the host rates?','Competitor effectively explained HOSA to the host','Competitor’s GPA','Competitor’s height','Competitor’s salary goals','There are five objectives.'),q('The learning outcomes summary may be:','One page, including the subtitle','Three pages','Any length','Two pages plus photos','One page only.'),q('While shadowing, an observer should:','Not provide patient care','Help lift patients','Give medications','Document in the chart','Observers watch and learn.'),q('Taking photos of patient records during shadowing is:','A privacy violation','Encouraged for the portfolio','Required by HOSA','Allowed if blurred later','Protect confidentiality.'),q('Which describes “who, what, where and when”?','Host, activity, setting and dates of the experience','The competitor’s school schedule','The judges’ names','The upload date','It sets the context.'),q('A Work-based Learning Form missing the host’s title earns:','2 points instead of 5','5 points','10 points','Bonus points','Incomplete forms lose points.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would arrange and complete a job-shadowing experience for your chosen career and then write a learning outcomes summary that earns full points.',rubric:[['Arranging and documenting',['8 hours','form','host','signature','title','location']],['Professional conduct',['explain hosa','punctual','attire','phone','professional']],['Confidentiality and safety',['hipaa','confidential','observer','photo','patient care']],['Summary criteria',['who','what','where','when','insight','learned','one page','align']]],model:'I would contact a local outpatient clinic weeks in advance, explain HOSA and the purpose, complete any confidentiality and immunization paperwork and schedule at least 8 hours, possibly over two visits. I would bring the Work-based Learning Form so the professional records dates, check-in and check-out times and total hours, their name, title and location, an evaluation of my performance and their signature. I would arrive early in professional attire, explain HOSA briefly, keep my phone away, ask prepared questions at appropriate moments and act only as an observer, never providing patient care or recording identifying information. My one-page learning outcomes summary, including its subtitle, would state who I shadowed, what I observed, where and when, explain insights about the work environment and teamwork with specific examples, describe what I learned—including how my selected skill is performed in practice—and explain how the experience confirmed my career choice.'},sources:[RULES,HIPAA,AHRQ_TEAM]};

export const S7={title:'Writing the skill checklist',hook:'Judges score your skill video against a checklist you write. If the checklist is vague, out of order or missing safety steps, they cannot award points for the performance—so the checklist is both a 10-point portfolio item and the blueprint for your video.',objectives:['Follow HOSA’s skill checklist template with a complete reference.','Write at least 10 clear, observable steps in logical sub-parts.','Include standard opening and closing steps such as hand hygiene, identification and documentation.','Evaluate a sample checklist against the rating sheet.'],sections:[
['The checklist template',[
'The checklist may be **one or two pages** and must follow HOSA’s template: competitor number and judge’s signature lines at the top; a **reference** with **title, author, copyright and page numbers**; the skill name; numbered steps (**minimum 10**); and a **judge-use comments column**. The skill must come from a **verifiable text resource**, follow that resource’s steps and be **typed**. Competitors may adapt the template but must keep its components.',
'The rating sheet awards 10 points when all four criteria are met: **(1)** the skill aligns with the chosen career, **(2)** it helps develop health career awareness, **(3)** the checklist includes **at least 10 steps from the text/resource** and **(4)** the skill is broken into **logical sub-parts including all necessary steps**. Missing or out-of-order steps drop the score to 4 or 2.']],
['Writing observable steps',[
'Each step should describe **one observable action** a judge can see and check: begin with a verb, be specific and keep critical safety details. “Prepare” is vague; “Select the largest speculum that fits comfortably into the ear canal” is observable. Break complex actions into **sub-parts** (4a, 4b, 4c) so judges can follow. Most clinical skills share a structure: **preparation** (hand hygiene, gather equipment, identify the patient, explain the procedure, obtain consent, provide privacy), **procedure** (the core steps from the text) and **completion** (patient comfort, clean and store equipment, hand hygiene, document and report).',
sort('Observable step or vague step?',['Observable and checkable','Too vague to judge'],[['Perform hand hygiene with alcohol-based rub for 20 seconds',0],['Get ready',1],['Identify the patient using name and date of birth',0],['Do the procedure correctly',1],['Explain the procedure and obtain verbal consent',0],['Be careful',1],['Dispose of the used speculum in the designated container',0],['Finish up',1]])]],
['A sample checklist: otoscopic inspection',[
'The steps below illustrate checklist structure for an **audiologist** competitor using otoscopic inspection. They summarize widely taught technique; a competitor must take their actual steps from the verifiable text they cite.',
{t:'table',caption:'Illustrative checklist structure (steps must come from your cited text)',head:['Step','Action'],rows:[['1','Perform hand hygiene and gather the otoscope and disposable specula'],['2','Identify the patient with two identifiers and introduce yourself'],['3','Explain the procedure and obtain verbal consent'],['4a','Ask about ear pain, drainage, recent surgery or hearing changes'],['4b','Inspect the outer ear and surrounding skin'],['5','Check that the otoscope light works and attach the largest speculum that fits comfortably'],['6','Position the patient seated with the head tilted slightly toward the opposite shoulder'],['7','For an adult, gently pull the pinna up and back to straighten the canal'],['8','Brace the hand holding the otoscope against the patient’s head'],['9','Insert the speculum gently into the outer canal while looking through the viewer'],['10','Inspect the canal and the eardrum, noting color, landmarks and any wax or drainage'],['11','Withdraw the otoscope gently and repeat on the other ear with a new speculum if indicated'],['12','Dispose of specula, clean the otoscope and perform hand hygiene'],['13','Document findings and report abnormal findings']]},
chk('Why does step 8 tell the competitor to brace their hand against the patient’s head?',['If the patient moves, the otoscope moves with them, preventing injury','To hold the patient still forcibly','To check the pulse','It is decorative'],'Bracing prevents the speculum from being pushed into the canal.')]],
['Common checklist errors',[
'Frequent problems include fewer than 10 steps; steps copied from a video or a person rather than a text; missing reference details; combining many actions in one step; steps out of the text’s order; omitting safety steps (identification, hand hygiene, infection control); and steps the competitor cannot perform on camera. Another error is choosing a skill whose steps are almost entirely verbal. Before finalizing, compare each line to the text and to the rating sheet, and have someone else read it and try to follow it.',
kase('Reviewing a draft checklist',{a:{text:'A draft checklist has eight steps; step 5 reads “Do the assessment.” What should the competitor fix first?',choices:[['Break step 5 into specific observable sub-steps from the text so there are at least 10 steps','b','Correct. Vague, combined steps hide the skill and the count is too low.',true],['Add two unrelated steps to reach 10','b','Steps must come from the text and be part of the skill.',false]]},b:{text:'The reference lists only a website name. What is needed?',choices:[['Title, author, copyright and page numbers from a verifiable text','c','Correct. The template requires all four.',true],['Nothing more','c','An incomplete reference weakens the checklist.',false]]},c:{text:'The revised checklist has 14 observable steps in logical sub-parts with a complete textbook reference.',end:true}})]],
['From checklist to performance',[
'The judges use your checklist to rate the video, so the **video must follow the same steps in the same order**. Practice with the checklist next to you until each step is automatic, then rehearse narrating key rationales briefly. Mark the steps you will highlight in the presentation if time does not allow the full video—usually the most career-specific and technically demanding ones. Keep a clean copy of the checklist and the text page open as you record.',
order('Moving from text to video',['Read the procedure in the verifiable text','Write numbered observable steps with sub-parts','Check the step count and reference details','Practice the skill with the checklist','Rehearse brief rationales for key steps','Record the video following the checklist exactly'])]],
["Adapting checklists to different careers",["Checklists look different across careers, but good ones share features. A **registered dietitian** competitor measuring waist circumference would include locating the top of the hip bone, keeping the tape horizontal and snug without compressing the skin, and reading at the end of a normal exhale, followed by interpreting the measurement and teaching. An **orthotist** competitor fitting a prefabricated wrist splint would include assessing skin and circulation, selecting the correct size, positioning the wrist as the text directs, securing straps, checking fit and circulation again and teaching wear and skin checks. An **occupational therapy** skill would focus on instructing and observing the patient performing a task. In each case, steps come from the cited text, and safety and communication steps bracket the procedure.","If your skill uses equipment you do not have, ask your CTE program, a community college or your shadowing site whether you can borrow it, or choose a different skill. Do not substitute equipment that changes the procedure. When the text includes variations (adult versus child, left versus right side), choose one and state it clearly on the checklist title, such as “Otoscopic Inspection—Adult Patient.”",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Preparation, procedure or completion step?","buckets":["Preparation","Completion"],"items":[["Perform hand hygiene and gather equipment",0],["Identify the patient and explain the procedure",0],["Clean and store equipment",1],["Document findings and report abnormalities",1],["Provide privacy",0],["Ensure the patient is comfortable before leaving",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Use the template: complete reference (title, author, copyright, pages), skill name, at least 10 numbered steps and a judges’ comments column.','Write one observable, verb-first action per step, in logical sub-parts, including preparation and completion steps.','The video must follow the checklist steps in the same order.'],terms:[['Skill checklist','A typed, step-by-step list judges use to rate a skill.'],['Sub-part','A lettered breakdown of a step, such as 4a and 4b.'],['Observable step','An action a judge can see and confirm.']],mcqs:[q('What is the minimum number of steps on the checklist?','10','5','20','8','The template requires at least 10.'),q('How long may the skill checklist be?','One to two pages','Five pages','Half a page','Unlimited','It is a one-to-two-page document.'),q('Which step is best written?','Perform hand hygiene with alcohol-based rub for 20 seconds','Get ready','Be careful','Finish up','It is specific and observable.'),q('The steps on the checklist must come from:','The verifiable text resource cited','A teacher’s memory','A classmate','Any video','Follow the steps in the resource.'),q('Why break a step into sub-parts?','For clarity so judges can follow each action','To make the checklist longer only','Because judges require letters','It is not allowed','Logical sub-parts are a rating criterion.'),q('In the sample otoscopy checklist, an adult’s pinna is pulled:','Up and back','Down and forward','Straight down','It is not moved','This straightens the adult canal.'),q('Judges use the checklist to:','Rate the overall skill performance in the video','Score the title page','Count references','Time the presentation','The checklist is the scoring guide.'),q('Which is a common checklist error?','Steps out of the text’s order','Including hand hygiene','Including documentation','A complete reference','Order matters.')],frq:{prompt:'Write the structure of a skill checklist for a clinical skill in your chosen career (you may describe the steps generally), and explain how it meets each of the four rating-sheet criteria.',rubric:[['Template components',['reference','title','author','copyright','page','comments']],['At least 10 observable steps',['10','step','hand hygiene','identify','explain','document']],['Logical sub-parts and order',['sub-part','4a','order','sequence','logical']],['Career alignment and awareness',['align','career','awareness','representative']]],model:'My checklist for otoscopic inspection as an audiologist follows HOSA’s template: competitor number and judge signature lines, a reference with the textbook’s title, author, copyright year and page numbers, the skill name, numbered steps and a judge comments column. Preparation steps include hand hygiene, gathering the otoscope and specula, identifying the patient, explaining the procedure and obtaining consent; the procedure includes asking about symptoms (4a) and inspecting the outer ear (4b), selecting the speculum, positioning the head, pulling the adult pinna up and back, bracing the hand, inserting gently, inspecting the canal and eardrum and withdrawing; completion includes disposal, cleaning, hand hygiene and documentation, for 13 or more observable steps in the text’s order. The skill aligns with the career because audiologists routinely inspect the ear before hearing testing, and it builds career awareness by showing a judge the careful, patient-centered assessment at the heart of the profession.'},sources:[RULES,NIDCD,EHC]};

export const S8={title:'Assembling and formatting the portfolio',hook:'Fifteen of the 85 portfolio points depend on format alone: neatness, the running header and fonts, and staying within 12 pages. One missing page number or a thirteenth page can cost points that hours of research earned. Formatting is the easiest place to get everything right.',objectives:['Apply HOSA’s formatting rules for font, spacing, margins and running header.','Order the portfolio sections with correct page limits.','Create a complete title page and an APA reference page.','Proofread and export one combined PDF for upload.'],sections:[
['Formatting rules',[
'Every portfolio page is **typed and single-sided**, in **12-point Arial**, **double-spaced**, in English, with **1-inch margins** on **8½ × 11-inch** paper. Each page has a **running header** with the competitor’s **last name and event name on the top left** and the **page number on the top right**—the title page is not counted. The formatting item (5 points) is all or nothing: every page must meet every requirement. Section headers are required, and content may be narrative or bulleted outline.',
'In a word processor, set the font and spacing for the whole document before writing, insert the header once using the header tools (with “different first page” so the title page has no header), and use automatic page numbers that start at 1 on the page after the title page. Avoid manual spacing tricks—they break when edits are made.',
"Tables, bullet lists and images follow the same rules: keep body text in 12-point Arial, keep tables inside the margins, and give each table or figure a short label if you refer to it. Photos are optional and do not add points—use them only where they help, such as a picture of equipment on the title page—and never include images of patients or identifiable people without permission. Save your work often with version names (portfolio-v1, v2) so an accidental deletion does not cost hours.",
"Uploading is a separate skill. HOSA’s digital upload instructions explain how to log in, select the event and attach the file. Name the PDF clearly (for example, Lastname_ClinicalSpecialty.pdf), check the file size limit, upload at least several days early to avoid last-minute system problems, and keep the confirmation. If your state conference also used the system, remember that a **separate ILC upload** is still required by May 15.",
chk('Which header meets HOSA’s rule?',['Top left: “Garcia – Clinical Specialty”; top right: page number','Top center: competitor’s full name only','Bottom right: page number only','Top left: school name; no page numbers'],'Last name and event top left, page number top right.')]],
['Sections and page limits',[
'The portfolio is a **maximum of 11 pages plus reference page(s)**, and the rating sheet’s maximum-pages item states that **no pages above 12 will be judged**. Sections must appear with headers:',
{t:'table',caption:'Portfolio sections and limits',head:['Section','Maximum length','Rating points'],rows:[['Title page (centered)','1 page','5'],['Career summary','2 pages','10'],['Education, training, credentialing, association and career data','2 pages','10'],['Interview summary','2 pages','10'],['Work-based Learning Form','1 page','5'],['Learning outcomes summary (including subtitle)','1 page','10'],['Skill checklist','2 pages','10'],['Reference page(s)','—','5']]},
order('Put the portfolio sections in order',['Title page','Career summary','Education, training, credentialing and career data','Interview summary','Work-based Learning Form','Learning outcomes summary','Skill checklist','Reference page(s)'])]],
['The title page',[
'The title page is **centered**, **one page only** and must include the **event name, competitor’s name, HOSA division, HOSA chapter number, school name, chartered association, chosen health profession and chosen skill**. A creative design or pictures may be used but will not affect the score. Missing any one item earns zero for the 5-point title page item, so check the list line by line.',
sort('Required on the title page?',['Required','Not required'],[['HOSA chapter number',0],['Chartered association',0],['Chosen skill',0],['HOSA division',0],['A photograph',1],['Competitor’s GPA',1],['Chosen health profession',0],['Advisor’s phone number',1]])]],
['References in APA style',[
'The reference page lists the literature that guided the portfolio. HOSA notes **APA** is the preferred style in health sciences; points are awarded for a **clean, legible reference page**, though its formatting is not judged in detail. Still, accurate APA references show professionalism. A book reference lists author, year, title in italics and publisher; a web page lists author or organization, date, title, site name and URL; an interview is a **personal communication**, cited in text but usually not listed on the reference page in APA. List references alphabetically with a hanging indent.',
match('Match the source to its APA element',[['Book title','Italicized, sentence case'],['Web page with no date','Use (n.d.)'],['Government agency author','Use the agency name as author'],['Personal interview','Cite in text as personal communication'],['Reference list order','Alphabetical by author'],['Second and later lines','Hanging indent']])]],
['Proofreading and exporting',[
'Neatness (5 points) deducts for formatting, grammar, spelling or appearance errors: 1–2 errors earn 4, 3–4 earn 3, 5–6 earn 2 and more than 6 earn zero. Proofread in several passes—content, then grammar and spelling, then formatting—read aloud and ask another person to review. Then insert the **scanned Work-based Learning Form** as an image page, export the entire document as **one PDF**, open the PDF to check every page, header and image, confirm the page count and upload to the HOSA Digital Upload System well before **May 15**.',
kase('Final check before upload',{a:{text:'Your exported PDF is 13 pages: title page, 11 content pages and a reference page. What should you do?',choices:[['Trim content so the total is within 12 pages','b','Correct. Pages above 12 will not be judged and the maximum-pages item would be lost.',true],['Upload it anyway','b','The maximum-pages item is all or nothing.',false]]},b:{text:'You notice the header shows page numbers on the title page. Fix?',choices:[['Use a different first-page header so numbering starts after the title page','c','Correct. The title page is not counted.',true],['Delete all page numbers','c','Page numbers are required on every page after the title page.',false]]},c:{text:'The final 12-page PDF has correct headers, fonts and margins, opens properly and is uploaded on May 8.',end:true}})]],
["Writing in a professional voice",["Judges read many portfolios, so clarity stands out. Write **concise, specific sentences**: replace “There are a lot of different kinds of things that respiratory therapists do” with “Respiratory therapists assess breathing, deliver aerosol medications and manage ventilators.” Use **consistent terminology** (choose “patient” or “client” according to the career and stay with it), define abbreviations the first time (“registered respiratory therapist (RRT)”) and spell medical terms correctly. Research sections usually use an objective, third-person voice; the interview summary and learning outcomes summary may use first person to describe your experience and insights.","Organize with clear **section headers** that match the rating-sheet names so judges can find each criterion quickly. Bullet points work well for education paths or duties, while narratives suit the interview and learning outcomes. Make scanned pages legible—scan in color or grayscale at good resolution, straighten the page and crop edges. Avoid decorative fonts or colored text that could be read as a formatting error; the title page is the place for design.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Strong or weak portfolio writing?","buckets":["Strong","Weak"],"items":[["“Sonographers use high-frequency sound waves to create images of organs.”",0],["“Sonography is like really cool and stuff.”",1],["Section header: “Interview Summary”",0],["Undefined abbreviation “RDMS” used repeatedly",1],["A crisp, straight scan of the signed form",0],["Colored decorative font in body text",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['12-point Arial, double-spaced, single-sided, 1-inch margins; header with last name and event top left, page number top right (title page not counted).','Maximum 11 pages plus references, no more than 12 judged; follow section limits.','Title page lists all eight items; references in APA; proofread and export one PDF.'],terms:[['Running header','Text repeated at the top of each page.'],['Hanging indent','Reference format with lines after the first indented.'],['Personal communication','APA citation type for interviews.']],mcqs:[q('What font and size are required?','12-point Arial','11-point Times New Roman','14-point Calibri','10-point Arial','Arial 12 pt, double-spaced.'),q('Where does the page number appear?','Top right of each page','Bottom center','Top left','Only on the title page','Last name and event appear top left.'),q('What is the maximum number of pages judged?','12','8','15','20','No pages above 12 will be judged.'),q('Which item is NOT required on the title page?','A photograph','Chapter number','Chosen skill','Chartered association','Pictures are optional and not scored.'),q('What is the preferred reference style?','APA','MLA','Chicago','None','APA is common in health sciences.'),q('How is the Work-based Learning Form included?','Scanned and inserted as a page in the single PDF','Mailed separately','Uploaded as a separate file','Brought to Round Two only','Everything must be one PDF.'),q('A portfolio with 3 neatness errors earns:','3 of 5 points','5 of 5','0 points','10 points','1–2 errors earn 4; 3–4 earn 3.'),q('Learning outcomes summary maximum length:','One page including subtitle','Two pages','Three pages','No limit','Stay within the limit.')],frq:{prompt:'Create a final formatting checklist for a Clinical Specialty portfolio that would protect every all-or-nothing point, and explain how you would export and verify the upload.',rubric:[['Formatting rules',['arial','12','double','1-inch','1 inch','single-sided','margin']],['Header and numbering',['header','last name','event','page number','top right','title page']],['Sections and limits',['12 pages','11','title page','two pages','one page']],['Export and verification',['one pdf','single pdf','proofread','may 15','open','check']]],model:'My checklist: every page typed and single-sided in 12-point Arial, double-spaced, with 1-inch margins on letter paper; a running header with my last name and “Clinical Specialty” on the top left and the page number on the top right of every page except the uncounted title page; section headers for every section. The title page is centered and lists the event, my name, division, chapter number, school, chartered association, chosen profession and skill. Sections stay within limits—two pages each for the career summary, education data, interview and checklist, one page for the form and learning outcomes—with the total, including references, no more than 12 pages. References are in APA and alphabetical. I would proofread in passes for content, grammar and formatting, have someone else review, insert the scanned Work-based Learning Form, export everything as one PDF, open it to verify every page and the page count and upload before May 15.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOAD,APA,APA_PAPER]};

export const S9={title:'Recording the skill video',hook:'The skill video is worth 15 points—the single largest item in Round Two—but it is never uploaded. Judges see only what you choose to play on your own device in a five-minute presentation, so the video must be clear, focused and ready to cue instantly.',objectives:['Meet the four video criteria on the Round Two rating sheet.','Plan lighting, sound, framing and angle so every checklist step is visible.','Perform the skill safely and exactly as written on the checklist.','Edit clips and prepare the device for a presentation without Wi-Fi or power.'],sections:[
['What the rating sheet rewards',[
'The video item awards 15 points when the recording **(1) contains high-quality audio**, **(2) has high-quality visual appearance and an effective angle**, **(3) shows the competitor performing the actual skill appropriately** and **(4) is utilized as intended during the presentation to reflect a deep understanding of the chosen career**. Meeting all four but less strongly earns 12; two earns 9; one, or a video where the competitor **is not seen**, or **too little shown to judge**, earns 6; not sharing the video earns zero. The video is **not uploaded or pre-judged**; it is shown on a battery-powered device during Round Two.',
sort('Which video criterion does each choice address?',['Audio or visual quality','Performance or use in the presentation'],[['Recording in a quiet room with a clip-on microphone',0],['Bright, even lighting from the front',0],['Competitor’s hands and face visible throughout',1],['Following the checklist steps exactly',1],['Explaining the rationale while the clip plays',1],['Camera on a tripod at the level of the work',0]])]],
['Planning the shoot',[
'Write a short shot plan from the checklist: which steps need a **wide shot** (positioning, patient interaction) and which need a **close-up** (hand placement, equipment settings). Use a **tripod** or stable surface, shoot **horizontally**, keep the light source **behind the camera** and avoid bright windows behind you. Choose a quiet space, turn off fans and notifications and use an external or clip-on microphone if possible. Wear professional attire or scrubs appropriate to the career, tie back hair and remove jewelry when the skill requires. Set up the area as a clinician would—equipment organized, clean surfaces, waste container ready.',
order('Prepare the recording',['Review the checklist and write a shot plan','Set up equipment and a clean work area','Arrange lighting from the front and a quiet space','Mount the camera on a tripod and test framing','Record a short test and check audio and focus','Perform the skill following the checklist','Review the footage against the checklist','Re-record any step that is unclear'])]],
['Performing safely and authentically',[
'The skill may use a **model or manikin** and may be **simulated**, but it must be performed, not just narrated, and it must be **safe**. Use a consenting volunteer only for noninvasive steps; never puncture skin, give medications or perform invasive procedures on a person. Perform every preparation and completion step—hand hygiene, identification, explanation, privacy, cleanup, documentation—because they are part of the skill and part of the career. Speak to your “patient” as a professional would. If you make an error, stop and restart the step or the take; judges rate what they see.',
kase('Retake or keep?',{a:{text:'Reviewing your video, you see that your hand blocks the camera during the most important step. What should you do?',choices:[['Re-record that segment with a better angle or a close-up','b','Correct. Judges must see the critical step to score it.',true],['Keep it and describe the step aloud','b','Verbalizing does not replace a visible performance.',false]]},b:{text:'In another take, you skipped hand hygiene at the end. What now?',choices:[['Re-record so the performance matches the checklist completely','c','Correct. Judges compare the video with your checklist.',true],['Leave it; judges will not notice','c','Completion steps are on your checklist and will be scored.',false]]},c:{text:'The final video shows every step clearly in order, with you visible and audio understandable.',end:true}})]],
['Editing and preparing clips',[
'You may show **all or part** of the video and may fast-forward, reverse or prepare **clips** to avoid searching. Because five minutes must also cover the career, the video portion is often one to two minutes. Edit to remove dead time, add simple step titles if helpful and keep the original full-length file as a backup. Choose clips that best show **competence**: the most technical, career-specific steps plus enough of the opening and closing steps to show safe practice. Do not add music that hides your voice.',
'Prepare the device: fully **charged** (HOSA provides **no electricity or Wi-Fi**), the video **downloaded locally** rather than streaming, notifications off, brightness high, volume tested and the clip **cued and ready to play**. The screen must be **visible to judges up to five feet away**, so a tablet or laptop is better than a small phone. Electronic notecards must be on a **different device**, and nothing besides the skill video may be shown to judges.',
chk('Your video is stored in a cloud folder. What is the risk?',['There is no Wi-Fi provided, so it may not play','Judges prefer streaming','It is required to stream','Cloud storage is automatically uploaded to HOSA'],'Download the video to the device in advance.')]],
['Using the video to show understanding',[
'The fourth criterion is about how you **use** the video. As it plays, briefly narrate why key steps matter, what a professional watches for and how the step protects the patient. Connect the skill to the career: how often professionals perform it, what decisions follow from it and which team members use its results. This turns a demonstration into evidence that you understand the profession.',
flip('Narration that shows understanding',[['“Here I brace my hand.”','“I brace my hand so that if the patient moves, the otoscope moves with them—this prevents canal injury.”'],['“Now I wash my hands.”','“Hand hygiene before and after protects both patients from ear infections spreading between them.”'],['“I look in the ear.”','“I check the eardrum’s color and landmarks; a red, bulging drum would lead me to refer the patient before hearing testing.”']])]],
["Presentation-day troubleshooting",["Technology fails at the worst moments, so plan for problems. Charge the device fully the night before and bring it at 100%; disable automatic updates and screen locks; keep the video in the device’s own storage and in a second app or folder on the same device in case one player fails; and practice opening the file in airplane mode to be sure it works offline. If you use clips, name them in order (01-prep, 02-procedure, 03-close) so you can find them instantly. Check the video’s orientation and that captions or step titles are readable at a distance.","In the room, ask the judges where they would like you to stand, angle the screen toward them and hold the device steady or set it on a stand at their eye level. Keep talking while the clip plays—your narration is part of the score. If the video will not play, stay calm, try your backup file quickly and, if it still fails, continue describing the skill while you troubleshoot only briefly; spending the whole presentation on technology costs more points than moving on. Afterward, note what went wrong so you can prevent it next time.",{"t":"check","q":"What is the best way to make sure the video works without Wi-Fi?","options":["Test it in airplane mode before the conference","Ask HOSA for the network password","Upload it to social media","Use a cloud link"],"why":"HOSA provides no Wi-Fi; test offline playback."}]]
],takeaways:['The 15-point video item rewards audio, visual quality and angle, the competitor visibly performing the skill and using it to show understanding.','Plan shots from the checklist, use a tripod, front lighting and a quiet room; perform safely on a model and re-record unclear steps.','Download clips locally to a charged device visible at five feet; narrate rationales during the presentation.'],terms:[['Shot plan','A list of camera angles needed for each step.'],['Clip','A short edited section of the video.'],['Rationale','The reason a step is performed.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the skill video item worth?','15','5','10','25','It is the largest Round Two item.'),q('Is the skill video uploaded for pre-judging?','No; it is shown during the presentation','Yes, by May 15','Yes, as a YouTube link','Only if requested','It is integrated into Round Two.'),q('A video where the competitor is not seen earns at most:','6 points','15 points','12 points','10 points','The competitor must be visible performing.'),q('What does HOSA provide for the video?','Nothing—no device, Wi-Fi or electricity','A projector','A laptop','Wi-Fi access','Bring a charged device with the video saved.'),q('How far away must judges be able to see the screen?','Up to five feet','Twenty feet','One foot','Across the room','A tablet or laptop works best.'),q('Which practice improves visual quality?','Lighting from the front with the camera on a tripod','Standing in front of a bright window','Handheld vertical filming while walking','Filming in a dark room','Stable, well-lit footage is clearer.'),q('If a critical step is blocked from view, you should:','Re-record it with a better angle','Describe it aloud instead','Skip it','Speed up the video','Judges must see the step.'),q('Which is allowed during the presentation?','Fast-forwarding to prepared clips','Showing slides on the same device','Showing notecards to judges','Streaming from Wi-Fi provided by HOSA','Clips save time.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would plan, record, edit and present your skill video to earn all 15 points.',rubric:[['Audio and visual quality',['audio','microphone','lighting','tripod','angle','quiet']],['Performance',['checklist','visible','safe','model','re-record','steps']],['Device and clips',['battery','charged','download','clip','five feet','wi-fi']],['Showing understanding',['narrat','rationale','why','career','understanding']]],model:'I would write a shot plan from my checklist, choosing wide shots for positioning and communication and close-ups for hand placement. I would record in a quiet room with a clip-on microphone, front lighting and the camera on a tripod, test audio and focus, then perform every checklist step in order on a model or consenting volunteer for noninvasive steps, staying visible and re-recording any unclear or missed step. I would edit one-to-two-minute clips showing the most career-specific steps plus safe opening and closing steps, keep the full video as a backup and download everything to a charged tablet with notifications off, since HOSA provides no Wi-Fi or electricity, making sure judges can see it from five feet. During the presentation I would narrate rationales—why I brace my hand or what an abnormal finding would mean—and connect the skill to the career, showing deep understanding.'},sources:[RULES,NIDCD,GRR]};

export const S10={title:'The five-minute presentation and the healthcare system',hook:'Five minutes: career, choice, strengths, healthcare system and the skill video. With the timekeeper calling one minute remaining and stopping you at five, every sentence must earn points. Planning the minutes is as important as the content.',objectives:['Structure a five-minute presentation that covers all four content criteria and the video.','Explain how a career fits into the healthcare system.','Relate four or more personal strengths to the career.','Deliver with strong voice, stage presence and diction.'],sections:[
['Planning the five minutes',[
'The Round Two content criteria are worth 40 points: **understanding of the career** (job responsibilities, training and how to access employment), **how the career was selected**, **relating personal strengths and preferences** and **how the career fits into the healthcare system**—10 points each. Delivery adds 15 points and the video 15. A sample time budget keeps everything in: about 1 minute on the career and employment, 30 seconds on how you chose it, 1 minute on strengths, 45 seconds on the healthcare system and 1.5 minutes on the skill video, leaving a short close.',
{t:'table',caption:'A sample five-minute plan',head:['Segment','Time','Purpose'],rows:[['Opening and the career','0:00–1:00','Responsibilities, training, credentials, how to find jobs'],['How you chose it','1:00–1:30','A specific story or experience'],['Strengths and preferences','1:30–2:30','Four or more specific connections'],['Healthcare system','2:30–3:15','Setting, team, patient journey, payment'],['Skill video','3:15–4:45','Key clips with rationale'],['Close','4:45–5:00','One-sentence summary of why this career']]},
chk('The timekeeper announces one minute remaining while you are still describing your strengths. What is the best move?',['Summarize quickly and move to the skill video','Keep talking about strengths in full','Skip the video entirely','Ask for more time'],'The video is 15 points; protect time for it.')]],
['Understanding the career and accessing employment',[
'Full points require explaining the job’s requirements, **how to prepare for training** and **how to access employment opportunities**. Many competitors forget the last part. Describe where jobs are found (hospitals, clinics, schools, home health, industry), how graduates find them (clinical rotations, professional association job boards, hospital career sites, networking during shadowing) and what makes candidates competitive (credentials, experience, soft skills). Use one or two statistics from your research to show demand.']],
['How the career fits into the healthcare system',[
'Judges want to know where your career sits in the larger system. Describe the **settings**—acute care hospitals, outpatient clinics, long-term care, home health, public health, rehabilitation and community programs. Explain the **team**: whom the professional receives referrals or orders from, who uses their results and how they communicate (handoffs, electronic health records, team meetings). Trace the **patient journey**: where a patient meets this professional and what happens before and after. Mention **payment and regulation**: services may be paid by private insurance, Medicare, Medicaid or out of pocket, and professionals work under state licensure and accreditation standards. Finally, link the career to **outcomes**: safety, prevention, access and quality.',
match('Match the system concept to an example for an audiologist',[['Referral source','Primary care provider refers a child who failed a hearing screen'],['Setting','Outpatient audiology clinic or hospital'],['Team collaboration','Works with ENT physicians and speech-language pathologists'],['Payment','Insurance or Medicaid covers diagnostic hearing tests'],['Prevention and public health','Newborn hearing screening programs'],['Outcome','Early hearing aids support language development']]),
flip('System questions to answer',[['Where?','Which settings employ this professional?'],['Who?','Who sends patients and who receives results?'],['When?','At what point in the patient’s journey?'],['How paid?','Which payers cover the service?'],['Why it matters?','What outcomes improve because of this role?']])]],
['Telling your story: selection and strengths',[
'The **selection** item rewards a thorough, detailed explanation of **why** you chose the career—an experience, a person, a moment of discovery—rather than a passing mention. The **strengths** item gives 10 points for **four or more strong connections** between your attributes and the job’s demands. Use your research and shadowing: “The respiratory therapists I shadowed made rapid adjustments during a code; my experience as a lifeguard trained me to stay calm and act quickly.” Combine personal **strengths** (calm, precise, compassionate) with **preferences** (patient contact, science, variety, shift work) so the connections feel genuine.',
sort('Strong or weak connection?',['Strong, specific connection','Weak or generic'],[['“I’ve volunteered weekly at a nursing home, and OT assistants coach older adults through daily tasks.”',0],['“I like helping people.”',1],['“I’m precise in chemistry lab, and medical lab scientists must measure exactly.”',0],['“It pays well.”',1],['“I enjoy explaining things as a math tutor, and dietitians spend much of their day teaching.”',0],['“My friend thinks I’d be good at it.”',1]])]],
['Delivery: voice, stage presence and diction',[
'Delivery earns 15 points across three items. **Voice**: speak loudly enough to be heard, vary rate and volume and pause for emphasis. **Stage presence**: stand with good posture, make eye contact with each judge, use purposeful gestures and show enthusiasm. **Diction, pronunciation and grammar**: choose precise words, pronounce medical terms correctly and avoid fillers such as “um,” “uh” and “you know.” Notecards are optional and may not be shown to judges. Rehearse with a timer at least five times, record yourself to spot fillers, and practice handling the device smoothly so the video starts instantly.',
kase('Final rehearsal',{a:{text:'In your timed rehearsal, you run 5 minutes 40 seconds, and the video segment is cut off. What should you change?',choices:[['Trim the career overview to the most important facts and keep the video clips','b','Correct. Protect the 15-point video and all four content items.',true],['Speak faster without cutting anything','b','Rushing hurts voice and diction scores.',false]]},b:{text:'A friend notes you said “um” 14 times. What helps?',choices:[['Pause silently instead of filling, and practice transitions until they are automatic','c','Correct. Planned transitions reduce fillers.',true],['Read word for word from your notecards','c','Reading lowers eye contact and stage presence.',false]]},c:{text:'Your next rehearsal finishes at 4:50 with clear transitions, strong eye contact and every rubric item covered.',end:true}})]],
["Levels of care and trends shaping careers",["Healthcare is often described by **levels of care**. **Primary care** is first-contact, ongoing care for common problems and prevention—family medicine, pediatrics and many nurse practitioners. **Secondary care** is specialist care, often by referral, such as cardiology or orthopedics. **Tertiary care** is highly specialized hospital care—intensive care units, cardiac surgery, trauma centers. **Quaternary care** is the most advanced and experimental, such as transplants. **Public health** works at the population level through prevention, surveillance and education. Placing your career on this map—“audiologists work in both primary screening programs and tertiary cochlear implant centers”—shows judges system-level thinking.","The system is changing, and careers change with it. **Telehealth** extends services to rural and home settings. **Workforce shortages** in nursing, behavioral health and many allied health fields create opportunity. **Value-based care** pays for outcomes rather than volume, rewarding prevention, coordination and patient education. **Interprofessional teams** share responsibility for safety, and organizations such as **The Joint Commission** accredit hospitals against quality standards. **Health equity** efforts aim to close gaps in access and outcomes. Mentioning one or two trends that affect your career—and how professionals are adapting—signals a deep understanding that sets top presentations apart.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the level of care to an example","pairs":[["Primary care","Annual checkup with a family physician"],["Secondary care","Referral to a cardiologist"],["Tertiary care","Cardiac surgery in a specialized hospital"],["Quaternary care","Organ transplantation"],["Public health","Community vaccination campaign"],["Telehealth","Video visit with a speech-language pathologist"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Content (40 points): career and employment access, how you chose it, four-plus strength connections and fit in the healthcare system.','Plan the minutes; protect time for the 15-point video; one-minute warning, stop at five.','Explain settings, team, patient journey, payment and outcomes; deliver with strong voice, presence and diction.'],terms:[['Healthcare system','The organizations, people, payers and rules that deliver health services.'],['Referral','Sending a patient to another professional for services.'],['Vocal filler','Sounds like “um” or “uh” that interrupt speech.']],mcqs:[q('How many points are the four content items worth together?','40','20','15','70','Each is worth 10.'),q('Which part of “understanding the career” do competitors often forget?','How to access employment opportunities','Job responsibilities','Training','The career name','Explain how to find jobs.'),q('When does the timekeeper give a warning?','When one minute remains','At two minutes','At the start','There is no warning','The presentation stops at five minutes.'),q('Which describes how a career fits into the healthcare system?','Settings, team collaboration, patient journey and payment','Only the salary','Only the competitor’s hobbies','The school’s mascot','Judges want system-level understanding.'),q('Full points for strengths require:','Four or more strong connections','One connection','Three weak connections','No connections','Specific links score highest.'),q('Notecards during the presentation:','Are optional and may not be shown to judges','Are required','Must be shown to judges','Must be on the video device','Electronic notes need a separate device.'),q('Which improves the diction score?','Avoiding vocal fillers and pronouncing terms correctly','Speaking in a monotone','Reading every word','Using slang','Clear, precise language.'),q('Which payer is an example in the U.S. healthcare system?','Medicare','A school club','A gym membership','A HOSA chapter','Medicare and Medicaid are public payers.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline your five-minute Clinical Specialty presentation with timing, and write the section explaining how your chosen career fits into the healthcare system.',rubric:[['Timed structure covering all items',['minute','career','select','strength','system','video']],['Career and employment access',['training','responsibil','employment','job','credential']],['Healthcare system explanation',['setting','team','referral','patient','payment','medicare','insurance','outcome']],['Delivery and timing strategy',['voice','eye contact','filler','rehearse','timer','one minute']]],model:'My plan: 0:00–1:00, the audiologist career—assessing hearing and balance, fitting hearing aids, the doctoral degree (Au.D.), state licensure and how graduates find jobs through externships and association job boards; 1:00–1:30, how I chose it after my brother’s hearing loss was found by a newborn screen; 1:30–2:30, four connections, such as patience from tutoring, precision in lab work, comfort with technology and enjoying teaching families; 2:30–3:15, the healthcare system; 3:15–4:45, otoscopy clips with narrated rationale; and a short close. Audiologists work in hospitals, outpatient clinics, schools and ENT practices. Patients are often referred by pediatricians after failed screens or by primary care providers; audiologists collaborate with ENT physicians and speech-language pathologists, share results through the health record and guide treatment. Their services are paid by private insurance, Medicaid or Medicare, under state licensure. They improve outcomes such as language development and safety. I would rehearse with a timer, avoid fillers, maintain eye contact and protect video time after the one-minute warning.'},sources:[RULES,CMS,AHRQ_TEAM]};
